WEBVTT


00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:07.469
Here's a cut and sew pillow that's fast,
fast, fast, and you can finish it in an

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:07.770
evening.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.540
We often think of cut 'n sew
for necklines in full garments.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:19.410
It's a great technique for odd shaped
pieces like this heart pillow as well.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:26.190
Working the stripes and shaping with
increases and decreases just didn't sound

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.270
like fun to me.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.100
So I just knit some yardage.

00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:35.115
Played with the stripes, cut out the shape
and put my serger and sewing machine to

00:00:35.115 --> 00:00:35.565
work.

00:00:37.035 --> 00:00:37.965
Let's get started.

00:00:40.115 --> 00:00:45.955
I've used the Knit it Now 14 inch pillow
template, used some scrap fabric and made

00:00:45.955 --> 00:00:47.245
a quick pillow form.

00:00:47.894 --> 00:00:53.715
I believe you can find heart-shaped
pillows ready made, but I was in a hurry

00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:54.525
as always.

00:00:56.085 --> 00:01:00.120
As with everything knitting, the
project starts with a swatch.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.920
I've determined that my gauge is four
stitches and six rows to the inch.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:12.360
Armed with my gauge, I now know that to
knit a 14 inch square to make my pillow,

00:01:13.289 --> 00:01:19.230
I'll need to cast on at least 56 stitches
and I'll have to knit at least 84 rows

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:19.830
total.

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:25.220
Now I've cast on a bit more to
give myself some seam to play with.

00:01:28.035 --> 00:01:33.675
I'm knitting stripes, but you could use
any stitch pattern that's part of the fun

00:01:33.675 --> 00:01:34.464
of cut 'n sew.

00:01:34.695 --> 00:01:38.475
You don't have to worry about
shaping while working in pattern.

00:01:39.804 --> 00:01:41.875
What about planning out the stripes?

00:01:42.324 --> 00:01:47.065
Well, I've been known to meticulously
plan out stripes and use the Fibonacci

00:01:47.065 --> 00:01:52.875
sequence, but this time I threw caution to
the wind and just changed colors randomly.

00:01:53.505 --> 00:01:54.315
What do you think?

00:01:56.265 --> 00:02:00.585
Now that I've knit the squares, I'm going
to give them a good steaming to set the

00:02:00.585 --> 00:02:04.855
stitches and take out as much as
a curl along the edges as I can.

00:02:05.715 --> 00:02:11.615
Since this is an acrylic yarn, I won't set
the iron down or kill the fabric, although

00:02:11.615 --> 00:02:13.805
for a pillow, maybe it wouldn't hurt.

00:02:16.225 --> 00:02:22.015
Next I'll draw around the template and
then serge and cut around the heart shape.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.470
Before I start serging, I'm going
to snip here to the cut line.

00:02:28.679 --> 00:02:33.749
Right at this point of the heart, this'll
make it easier to serge the shape.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.810
We'll reinforce this
little area in a minute.

00:02:37.609 --> 00:02:42.260
If you don't have a serger user sewing
machine and stitch a single straight

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:48.170
stitch around your Mark, and then two
rounds of zigzag or a stretchy stitch,

00:02:48.565 --> 00:02:54.085
varying the width of that stitch to secure
the edges and then cut out the shape.

00:02:56.315 --> 00:03:00.665
If you haven't done this before, just
take a get deep breath and go for it.

00:03:01.505 --> 00:03:06.945
If you're really a scaredy cat, test
this out on your swatch first to get

00:03:06.945 --> 00:03:07.665
confidence.

00:03:12.015 --> 00:03:17.385
I'm using a serger that has a differential
feed, so I won't be getting an ugly

00:03:17.385 --> 00:03:18.525
lettuce edge.

00:03:19.204 --> 00:03:24.215
Be careful and test your serger stitch
settings first so you don't end up with

00:03:24.215 --> 00:03:24.725
this.

00:03:26.545 --> 00:03:29.274
Here are my pieces
straight off the serger.

00:03:30.285 --> 00:03:33.465
I might use a shot of steam
to flatten them or maybe not.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.609
This point of the heart's going to be the
most fragile, and I'm going to take time

00:03:40.609 --> 00:03:43.519
to reinforce that point
with the zigzag stitch.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.280
This'll be hidden in the seam so
you don't have to be too careful.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.690
Just secure that area so those
center stitches won't pop out later.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:04:02.190
Are we ready to assemble ? With the public
sides of the pieces facing each other pin

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:07.109
them together or run a quick basting
stitch by hand to secure the edges,

00:04:09.269 --> 00:04:14.579
join them together, and sew up the heart
shape, leaving an opening of six to eight

00:04:14.579 --> 00:04:16.950
inches to insert the pillow form.

00:04:33.525 --> 00:04:37.245
Once you get everything all
settled, close up that opening.

00:04:37.275 --> 00:04:41.145
I like to use the yarn
to close up the opening.

00:04:41.985 --> 00:04:44.755
Use a combination of a whip
stitch and a back stitch.

00:04:44.785 --> 00:04:47.365
Whatever's going to keep
that opening secure.

00:04:48.145 --> 00:04:48.745
And that's it.

00:04:50.185 --> 00:04:56.755
This is a quick, fun, colorful project to
celebrate Valentine's day or anytime you

00:04:56.755 --> 00:04:58.555
want to send a hug to someone.

00:05:00.835 --> 00:05:06.085
Now that we have a little experience and
we're not afraid to cut, let's think about

00:05:06.085 --> 00:05:08.285
making a whole garment with cut 'n sew.

